<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"><title>10.1 What is RAG? — AI Practitioner Associate</title><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Fraunces:ital,opsz,wght,SOFT@0,9..144,300..800,0..100;1,9..144,300..800,0..100https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Serif+Display:ital@0;1&family=DM+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,700;1,400&family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700&family=Sora:wght@400;600;700;800&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swapfamily=Instrument+Serif:ital@0;1https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Serif+Display:ital@0;1&family=DM+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,700;1,400&family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700&family=Sora:wght@400;600;700;800&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swapfamily=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Serif+Display:ital@0;1&family=DM+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,700;1,400&family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700&family=Sora:wght@400;600;700;800&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swapfamily=DM+Serif+Display:ital@0;1https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Serif+Display:ital@0;1&family=DM+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,700;1,400&family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700&family=Sora:wght@400;600;700;800&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swapfamily=DM+Sans:ital,opsz,wght@0,9..40,400..700;1,9..40,400..700https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Serif+Display:ital@0;1&family=DM+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,700;1,400&family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700&family=Sora:wght@400;600;700;800&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swapdisplay=swap" rel="stylesheet"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/course-styles.css"></head><body><div class="progress-circle-wrap"><svg viewBox="0 0 36 36"><circle class="progress-circle-track" cx="18" cy="18" r="15"/><circle class="progress-circle-fill" cx="18" cy="18" r="15" id="progressCircle"/></svg><div class="progress-circle-label" id="progressLabel">0%</div></div><nav class="topnav"><div class="topnav-inner"><a href="https://sustainsys.co.uk" class="nav-logo"><img src="../../assets/logo_only.png" alt="SustainSys" class="nav-logo-img"><div class="nav-logo-text">Sustain<span>Sys</span></div></a><div class="nav-divider"></div><div class="nav-title">Module 10 — What is RAG?</div><span class="nav-badge">Associate</span><a href="index.html" class="nav-home-link">← Course Home</a></div></nav><aside class="sidebar"><div class="sidebar-course-title">AI Practitioner Associate</div><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 10: RAG Pipelines</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson1" class="active"><span class="sidebar-num">10.1</span> What is RAG?</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">10.2</span> Document Chunking</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">10.3</span> Embeddings &amp; Vectors</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">10.4</span> Building RAG</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">10.5</span> RAG Evaluation</a></li></ul></aside><button class="sidebar-toggle">☰</button><main class="main"><div class="container"><section class="hero"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="index.html">Associate</a><span class="sep">›</span><span>What is RAG?</span></div><span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 10 · Lesson 1</span><h1>What is RAG?</h1><p class="hero-sub">Understand Retrieval-Augmented Generation: the technique that solves LLM limitations by injecting relevant documents into prompts before generation. Learn how RAG combats hallucination, enables access to current information, and powers practical LLM applications in production environments.</p></section><div class="objectives"><h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Understand what Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is and why it matters</li><li>Know the RAG pipeline architecture: retrieve, augment, and generate components</li><li>Learn how RAG solves hallucination and knowledge cutoff problems</li><li>Understand how retrieval quality impacts generation quality</li><li>Know common RAG implementations, tools, and frameworks</li><li>Design RAG systems for your specific use cases and domains</li><li>Implement a basic RAG workflow using OpenAI and Python</li></ul></div><section class="section" id="rag-intro"><span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span><h2>What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation?</h2><p>Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a technique that augments Large Language Models (LLMs) with external knowledge sources. Instead of relying solely on what an LLM learned during training, RAG first retrieves relevant documents from a knowledge base, then provides these documents to the LLM to generate responses. This approach dramatically reduces hallucination, enables access to information beyond the training cutoff date, and allows LLMs to reason about domain-specific knowledge they may not have been explicitly trained on.</p><p>The fundamental insight behind RAG is simple but powerful: <strong>Large Language Models are excellent at reasoning but poor at precise memorization and knowledge retrieval.</strong> By separating the knowledge storage (documents in a database) from the reasoning engine (the LLM), we can build systems that leverage the strengths of both components.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Simple RAG pipeline with OpenAI</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">openai</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">OpenAI</span>

<span class="va">client</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">OpenAI</span>()

<span class="cm"># Step 1: Retrieve relevant documents from your knowledge base</span>
<span class="va">query</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"What is the return policy for electronics?"</span>
<span class="va">retrieved_docs</span> <span class="op">=</span> [
    <span class="st">"Return policy: Electronics can be returned within 30 days of purchase if unopened and in original packaging. Opened electronics have a 15-day return window."</span>,
    <span class="st">"Refund processing: Refunds are processed within 5-7 business days after return receipt. Original shipping costs are non-refundable."</span>,
    <span class="st">"Special items: Custom or clearance electronics cannot be returned. All refunds exclude applicable taxes."</span>
]

<span class="cm"># Step 2: Augment the prompt with retrieved context</span>
<span class="va">context</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"\n\n"</span>.<span class="fn">join</span>(<span class="va">retrieved_docs</span>)
<span class="va">system_message</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">f"""You are a helpful customer service assistant.
Answer questions based on the provided company policies.
Be concise and accurate. If information is not in the provided context, say so.

COMPANY POLICIES:
{context}"""</span>

<span class="cm"># Step 3: Generate response using the augmented context</span>
<span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">client</span>.<span class="fn">chat</span>.<span class="fn">completions</span>.<span class="fn">create</span>(
    <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>,
    <span class="va">messages</span><span class="op">=</span>[
        {<span class="st">"role"</span>: <span class="st">"system"</span>, <span class="st">"content"</span>: <span class="va">system_message</span>},
        {<span class="st">"role"</span>: <span class="st">"user"</span>, <span class="st">"content"</span>: <span class="va">query</span>}
    ]
)

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">response</span>.<span class="fn">choices</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>].<span class="fn">message</span>.<span class="fn">content</span>)</code></pre><div class="key-concept"><strong>Key Insight:</strong> RAG separates knowledge (documents in a database) from reasoning (LLM intelligence). Models are terrible at memorizing facts but excellent at reasoning with provided information. RAG uses each component for what it does best.</div></section><section class="section" id="why-rag"><span class="section-label">Problem Solving</span><h2>Why RAG Matters: Solving LLM Limitations</h2><p>Large Language Models, despite their impressive capabilities, suffer from several critical limitations. RAG directly addresses these limitations and has become essential for production LLM applications.</p><div class="compare-grid"><div class="compare-item"><div class="compare-title">Hallucination Problem</div><div class="compare-content"><strong>Without RAG:</strong> LLMs generate plausible-sounding but false information when uncertain. <strong>With RAG:</strong> Responses are grounded in retrieved documents, reducing fabrication.</div></div><div class="compare-item"><div class="compare-title">Knowledge Cutoff</div><div class="compare-content"><strong>Without RAG:</strong> LLMs can only use knowledge up to their training date. <strong>With RAG:</strong> Can reference real-time or constantly updated documents.</div></div><div class="compare-item"><div class="compare-title">Domain Expertise</div><div class="compare-content"><strong>Without RAG:</strong> Generic knowledge may not apply to specialized domains. <strong>With RAG:</strong> Can incorporate domain-specific documents and terminology.</div></div><div class="compare-item"><div class="compare-title">Explainability</div><div class="compare-content"><strong>Without RAG:</strong> Hard to trace why model said something. <strong>With RAG:</strong> Can show which documents influenced the response.</div></div></div><p><strong>Real-world impact:</strong> In customer service, RAG enables accurate responses to questions about company policies even when those policies change daily. In healthcare, RAG allows LLMs to reference current clinical guidelines and patient records. In legal applications, RAG grounds responses in actual contracts and precedents.</p></section><section class="section" id="pipeline"><span class="section-label">Architecture</span><h2>The RAG Pipeline Architecture</h2><p>RAG systems follow a consistent three-step architecture. Understanding each step is crucial for building effective systems.</p><svg viewBox="0 0 900 350" style="width:100%; max-width:850px; margin:30px auto; display:block;"><defs><marker id="arrowhead" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="7" refX="9" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><polygon points="0 0, 10 3.5, 0 7" fill="#333"/></marker></defs><g><rect x="30" y="80" width="140" height="100" fill="#e8f4f8" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/><text x="100" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" font-size="14">User Query</text><text x="100" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">"How do I return items?"</text><text x="100" y="160" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#666">(Natural language)</text><path d="M 170 130 L 220 130" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/><rect x="220" y="60" width="160" height="140" fill="#fff4e6" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/><text x="300" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" font-size="14">Retrieval</text><text x="300" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Search vector database</text><text x="300" y="145" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Find similar documents</text><text x="300" y="165" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Rank by relevance</text><text x="300" y="185" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#666">(k top results)</text><path d="M 380 130 L 430 130" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/><rect x="430" y="60" width="160" height="140" fill="#e8f8e8" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/><text x="510" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" font-size="14">Augmentation</text><text x="510" y="125" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Format retrieved docs</text><text x="510" y="145" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Build context window</text><text x="510" y="165" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Create prompt</text><text x="510" y="185" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#666">(context + query)</text><path d="M 590 130 L 640 130" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/><rect x="640" y="80" width="140" height="100" fill="#f4e8f8" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/><text x="710" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold" font-size="14">Generation</text><text x="710" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">LLM generates</text><text x="710" y="160" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">answer based on</text><text x="710" y="180" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">context</text><path d="M 780 230 L 780 270" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/><rect x="680" y="270" width="130" height="60" fill="#e8e8f8" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/><text x="745" y="295" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">Final Answer</text><text x="745" y="315" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">(grounded, accurate)</text></g></svg><div class="term-grid"><div class="term-item"><div class="term-name">Step 1: Retrieval</div><div class="term-desc">Query a vector database using semantic similarity. Convert user question into embeddings, find the most similar documents. Quality determines what information is available to the LLM.</div></div><div class="term-item"><div class="term-name">Step 2: Augmentation</div><div class="term-desc">Format and combine retrieved documents with the original query into a single prompt. Context order, formatting, and completeness all matter here.</div></div><div class="term-item"><div class="term-name">Step 3: Generation</div><div class="term-desc">Feed augmented prompt to LLM. Model generates response using both the context and its reasoning abilities. Response quality depends on earlier steps.</div></div></div><section class="section" id="components"><span class="section-label">Key Components</span><h2>Essential RAG Components</h2><p>A complete RAG system requires several components working together. Each is critical to overall performance:</p><div class="subsection"><h3>Knowledge Base</h3><p>The collection of documents that your RAG system will search. This could be company documentation, customer manuals, research papers, or any domain-specific content. The knowledge base is typically stored as embeddings (numerical vectors) in a vector database like Chromadb, Pinecone, or Weaviate.</p></div><div class="subsection"><h3>Embedding Model</h3><p>Converts text (both documents and queries) into numerical vectors that capture semantic meaning. Models like OpenAI's text-embedding-3-small or Sentence Transformers create these vectors. Documents and queries in the same semantic space will have similar vectors.</p></div><div class="subsection"><h3>Vector Database (Vector Store)</h3><p>Stores and indexes embeddings for fast retrieval. Unlike traditional databases, vector databases use distance metrics (cosine similarity, Euclidean distance) to find similar items. Common choices include Chromadb (local), Pinecone (managed), and Milvus (open source).</p></div><div class="subsection"><h3>LLM (Large Language Model)</h3><p>Generates the final response. The LLM receives the augmented prompt and generates text conditioned on both the context and its trained knowledge. Can be OpenAI's GPT-4, Anthropic's Claude, open-source models like Llama, or others.</p></div></section><section class="section" id="workflow"><span class="section-label">Implementation</span><h2>Building a RAG System: Step-by-Step</h2><p>Here's a practical example showing how to build a RAG system from scratch using LangChain, Chromadb, and OpenAI:</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Complete RAG system with LangChain</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain</span>.<span class="im">document_loaders</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">TextLoader</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain</span>.<span class="im">text_splitter</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">CharacterTextSplitter</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain</span>.<span class="im">embeddings</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">OpenAIEmbeddings</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain</span>.<span class="im">vectorstores</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">Chroma</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain</span>.<span class="im">chat_models</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">ChatOpenAI</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain</span>.<span class="im">chains</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">RetrievalQA</span>

<span class="cm"># 1. Load documents</span>
<span class="va">loader</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">TextLoader</span>(<span class="st">"company_policies.txt"</span>)
<span class="va">documents</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">loader</span>.<span class="fn">load</span>()

<span class="cm"># 2. Split into chunks</span>
<span class="va">splitter</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">CharacterTextSplitter</span>(<span class="va">chunk_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1000</span>, <span class="va">chunk_overlap</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">200</span>)
<span class="va">chunks</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">splitter</span>.<span class="fn">split_documents</span>(<span class="va">documents</span>)

<span class="cm"># 3. Create embeddings and vector store</span>
<span class="va">embeddings</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">OpenAIEmbeddings</span>()
<span class="va">vector_store</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">Chroma</span>.<span class="fn">from_documents</span>(
    <span class="va">documents</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">chunks</span>,
    <span class="va">embedding</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">embeddings</span>,
    <span class="va">persist_directory</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"./chroma_db"</span>
)

<span class="cm"># 4. Create RAG chain</span>
<span class="va">llm</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">ChatOpenAI</span>(<span class="va">model_name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>, <span class="va">temperature</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0</span>)
<span class="va">rag_chain</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">RetrievalQA</span>.<span class="fn">from_chain_type</span>(
    <span class="va">llm</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">llm</span>,
    <span class="va">chain_type</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"stuff"</span>,
    <span class="va">retriever</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">vector_store</span>.<span class="fn">as_retriever</span>()
)

<span class="cm"># 5. Ask questions</span>
<span class="va">query</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"What is our return policy for defective items?"</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">rag_chain</span>.<span class="fn">run</span>(<span class="va">query</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">result</span>)</code></pre><section class="section" id="use-cases"><span class="section-label">Applications</span><h2>Real-World RAG Use Cases</h2><p>RAG has become essential across industries. Here are common applications:</p><div class="subsection"><h3>Customer Support Automation</h3><p>Enable chatbots to answer questions by retrieving relevant company policies, product documentation, and previous customer interactions. Reduces support costs while improving consistency and accuracy.</p></div><div class="subsection"><h3>Internal Knowledge Management</h3><p>Index company documentation, research papers, and internal memos. Employees can ask questions in natural language and get answers grounded in official company knowledge.</p></div><div class="subsection"><h3>Legal and Compliance</h3><p>Build systems that reference specific contracts, regulatory documents, and compliance guidelines. Critical for ensuring accuracy and traceability in regulated industries.</p></div><div class="subsection"><h3>Medical and Scientific Research</h3><p>Index clinical guidelines, research papers, and patient records. Researchers and clinicians can ask questions with responses grounded in current scientific evidence.</p></div><div class="subsection"><h3>Product Documentation</h3><p>Help customers quickly find answers from product manuals and FAQ documents. Especially valuable for complex products with extensive documentation.</p></div><div class="subsection"><h3>Real-Time News and Events</h3><p>Combine LLMs with up-to-date news documents to answer questions about current events beyond the model's training data.</p></div></section><section class="section" id="summary"><span class="section-label">Summary</span><h2>Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><strong>RAG Definition:</strong> Retrieval-Augmented Generation is a three-step process: retrieve relevant documents, augment the prompt with context, and generate responses using the LLM.</li><li><strong>Core Problem:</strong> LLMs hallucinate without grounding and can't access information beyond their training cutoff. RAG solves both problems.</li><li><strong>Architecture:</strong> RAG requires a knowledge base, embedding model, vector database, and LLM working together in a coordinated pipeline.</li><li><strong>Practical Value:</strong> RAG enables accurate, current, domain-specific LLM responses while remaining explainable by showing source documents.</li><li><strong>Implementation:</strong> Tools like LangChain, Chromadb, and OpenAI make building RAG systems accessible to most developers.</li><li><strong>Quality Factors:</strong> Success depends on document quality, chunking strategy, embedding model choice, and LLM reasoning ability.</li></ul></div><div class="exercise-box"><h3>Exercise: RAG Design Challenge</h3><p>Think about a domain you're familiar with. Design a RAG system for that domain:</p><ol><li>What documents would constitute your knowledge base?</li><li>How would you chunk these documents for optimal retrieval?</li><li>What embedding model would work best for your documents?</li><li>How would you evaluate if the retrieved documents are actually relevant?</li><li>What metrics would you track to ensure the system stays accurate over time?</li></ol><p><strong>Reflection:</strong> Write a brief summary of your RAG system design and consider what challenges you'd face implementing it.</p></div></section><hr style="border: none; height: 1px; background: rgba(54,207,201,0.1); margin: 40px 0;"><section class="section" id="rag-challenges"><span class="section-label">Real-World Challenges</span><h2>Common RAG Challenges and Practical Solutions</h2><p>Production RAG systems face unexpected challenges that academic examples don't address. Understanding these common failure modes helps you build robust systems that work reliably.</p><h3>Challenge: Hallucinations in Retrieved Context</h3><p>Even when documents are retrieved correctly, LLMs sometimes "hallucinate" by adding information not present in those documents. This happens because LLMs are trained to be fluent and confident, not just faithful to context.</p><p><strong>Solutions:</strong> (1) Require explicit citations linking to retrieved documents. (2) Add strict instructions: "Only use information in the provided documents. If information isn't present, say 'I don't know.'" (3) Use lower temperature settings (more deterministic). (4) Post-process responses to verify claims against source documents.</p><h3>Challenge: Stale or Outdated Knowledge</h3><p>Knowledge bases become outdated. Policies change, products evolve, facts become wrong. Real users notice immediately when answers reference outdated information, damaging trust in your system.</p><p><strong>Solutions:</strong> (1) Add document timestamps and prefer recent documents. (2) Quarterly audits: test the system against known-updated facts. (3) Version control your knowledge base. (4) Monitor user feedback for "that's wrong" signals. (5) Automated alerts when key documents haven't been updated recently.</p><h3>Challenge: Irrelevant Retrieval</h3><p>Sometimes semantic similarity doesn't match what users actually need. A query about "refunds" might retrieve documents about "returns" which are different processes. Keyword search alone fails too—synonyms aren't matched.</p><p><strong>Solutions:</strong> (1) Hybrid search combining keyword (BM25) and semantic (vector) search. (2) Iterate chunking strategy—different chunk sizes retrieve different content. (3) Improve embedding models—domain-specific models outperform general ones. (4) Add metadata filtering to restrict search scope. (5) User feedback: "Was this answer helpful?" signals which retrievals worked.</p><h3>Challenge: Context Window Limits</h3><p>Modern LLMs have finite context windows (typically 4K-100K tokens). Retrieve too many documents and you'll exceed the limit, forcing truncation. Truncate poorly and you lose critical information.</p><p><strong>Solutions:</strong> (1) Retrieve conservatively: top-3 documents, not top-20. (2) Document compression: summarize retrieved documents before passing to LLM. (3) Hierarchical retrieval: retrieve summaries first, then full documents. (4) Monitor token usage; warn when approaching limits.</p><h3>Challenge: Slow Query Latency</h3><p>RAG has multiple sequential steps: retrieve documents, generate embeddings, call LLM, generate response. Each step adds latency. Slow responses destroy user experience in interactive applications.</p><p><strong>Solutions:</strong> (1) Parallel retrieval: search multiple sources simultaneously. (2) Caching: cache embeddings and previous query results. (3) Asynchronous processing: return partial answers while processing continues. (4) Optimize embedding models—smaller models are faster (text-embedding-3-small vs. text-embedding-3-large). (5) Quantize vectors: reduce 4-byte floats to 1-byte integers for faster search.</p><div class="nav-buttons"><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson5" class="nav-btn prev"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M15 18l-6-6 6-6"/></svg> Previous</a><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson2" class="nav-btn next">Next <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></div></div></main><footer><div class="container"><div class="footer-logo"><img src="../../assets/logo_only.png" alt="SustainSys" class="footer-logo-img"><div class="footer-logo-text">Sustain<span>Sys</span> AI Academy</div></div><p>© 2026 SustainSys.</p><p><a href="https://sustainsys.co.uk">sustainsys.co.uk</a></p></div></footer><script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@supabase/supabase-js@2/dist/umd/supabase.min.js"></script>
